{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015ECx5T2LqU25SVFcPeBxur","type":"tool_result","content":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md:1005:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md:1082:**Name the pattern, because you will retrieve it by name:** *the object exists but nothing happens without the component.* You will meet this exact shape at least three more times in this book. Each time, the first instinct will be \"something is misconfigured,\" and each time the answer will be \"something is not installed.\"\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md:1436:Between \"this Pod object exists\" and \"this container is executing on a machine\" sits a decision nobody in this chapter made. Something has to look at a Pod with no node assigned to it, look at every node in the cluster, and choose. It has to know how much memory the Pod asked for and how much each node has left. It has to respect that some nodes are reserved, some are draining, and some are deliberately hostile to workloads that have not been told they are welcome.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:601:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:725:- **C is wrong** — and this is the useful confusion to clear up. Something can be *near-universal in practice* and still *not part of the cluster's definition*. The gap between those two is exactly where the absent-component pattern lives.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:742:✓ The addon/component distinction, and the absent-component pattern\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:1081:C) Nothing happens — the object exists as a description, but nothing acts on it\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:1225:**Q14 — C.** The object exists as a stored description; something has to be watching for it and willing to act, and if that component is absent, nothing acts. This is the absent-component pattern from §4.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-03-the-ship-s-company.md:1302:| **Absent-component pattern** | An object without its component does nothing. The description exists; nothing acts on it. |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-04-records-of-intent.md:284:> A Kubernetes object is a **\"record of intent\"** — once you create the object, the Kubernetes system will constantly work to ensure that the object exists [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23].\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:112:    - \"retrieval from Ch 3 §4 — the absent-component rule stated back in the reader's own words\"\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:151:    - \"absent-component-pattern\"\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:286:6. **An object without its component does nothing.** You have met it at least twice: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service on a cluster with no load balancer to provision, and a Service whose selector matched no Pods.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:341:*You'll also acquire one rule that outlives this chapter: an object without its component does nothing. You'll use it in Chapter 13, in Chapter 17, and on things this book never gets to.*\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:628:**An object without its component does nothing.**\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:641:> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** Chapter 3's phrase, verbatim, and worth writing on something you will see again: **an object without its component does nothing.** You have now personally met three instances: the LoadBalancer with no provider, the Service with no matching Pods, and this. Three sightings of the same light, and you stop calling it a coincidence and start calling it a landmark.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:714:**3. No Ingress controller is installed. `kubectl get` proves only that the object exists.**\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:730:**5. `[retrieval: ch3]` An object without its component does nothing.** The two Chapter 9 instances: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service on a cluster with no load balancer to provision one, and a Service whose selector matches no Pods.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1354:> Chapter 3 gave you the sentence — *an object without its component does nothing* — and told you that you would meet it four more times. This is where that debt comes due in full.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1394:           An object without its component does nothing.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1574:A) *An object without its component does nothing.* Instances: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service with no provider to fulfill it (Ch 9 §3); a Service whose selector matches no Pods (Ch 9 §4); an Ingress with no Ingress controller (Ch 10 §3); a NetworkPolicy on a plugin that does not implement NetworkPolicy (Ch 10 §7).\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1575:B) *An object without its component does nothing.* Instances: an Ingress with no Ingress controller, and a NetworkPolicy on a plugin that does not implement NetworkPolicy.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1577:D) *An object without its component does nothing.* Instances: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service with no provider; an Ingress with no controller; a NetworkPolicy on an unsupporting plugin; an Ingress whose `pathType` does not match the request path.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1802:*An object without its component does nothing.* Four instances so far:\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1856:| **The rule** | An object without its component does nothing. Four instances of the pattern so far. Ask: *what is watching this, and is it installed?* |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-10-traffic-from-beyond-the-cluster.md:1891:     that this is the chapter's third distinct \"four\" (§3 and §8 carry the absent-component-\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-12-locks-keys-and-watchstanders.md:1613:Everything above happens before an object exists. **Falco** answers a different question at a different time.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-12-locks-keys-and-watchstanders.md:1642:| **When** | before the object exists | after it is running |\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:37:#-- Ch 10 §3 (the absent-component pattern). A reader who skipped any ONE\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:212:    - \"absent-component-pattern\"\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:811:You do. The phrase is Chapter 3's, and Chapter 10 §3 named it as a pattern: **an object without its component does nothing** *[cross-bearing: see Ch 10 §3 — the object is not the implementation]*.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:815:This is the third sighting of the same light. An Ingress with no Ingress controller routes nothing. A NetworkPolicy on a CNI plugin that doesn't implement policy blocks nothing. A StorageClass with no running provisioner creates nothing. Each time, the object exists, `kubectl get` shows it, `kubectl describe` shows a sensible spec, and nothing happens, because the object was always only ever a request addressed to a component that has to be separately installed and running.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:1123:**An object without its component does nothing** *[cross-bearing: see Ch 10 §3 — the object is not the implementation]*. A StorageClass naming a CSI driver nobody deployed is not an exotic failure you have to imagine. It is the documented prerequisite, unmet. The claim sits unbound. The Pod sits `Pending`. `kubectl get storageclass` shows the class, healthy and correct, describing a capability that does not exist.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:1305:**2 — C.** *To use a CSI driver from a storage provider, you must first deploy it to your cluster* [source: k8s-glossary-storage-terms-2026-08-25], and *the core of Kubernetes does not install that software for you* [source: k8s-docs-volumes-csi-and-subpath-2026-08-25]. The object exists; the component does not; nothing happens.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:1473:A) The absent-component pattern — \"an object without its component does nothing\"; the claim remains unbound\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-11-below-the-waterline.md:1630:You now hold all four pluggable interfaces, and with them a rule you can state without help: an object without its component does nothing. You have watched that rule fire at the Ingress controller, at the network plugin, at the provisioner, and at the CSI driver — four sightings, one light. Chapter 17 will collect all four interfaces in one place and ask you what they have in common; you already know."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8899959a-2d32-4641-ba38-45cae6a1cb65","uuid":"df35fa49-f093-45a3-88fc-a3f5a155124d","timestamp":"2026-08-31T07:58:36.939Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md:1005:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md:1082:**Name the pattern, because you will retrieve it by name:** *the object exists but nothing happens without the component.* You will meet this exact shape at least three more times